WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
Neo4j is a powerful graph database that enables organizations to model, store, and analyze

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.460
complex relationships between data points.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.800
Its intuitive, graph-based structure offers a flexible and efficient solution for diverse

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.320
applications, making it a leading choice for managing connected data.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.760
This course is taught by FreeCoCamp team members Farhan Chaudhary and Gavin Lon.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.360
First, you will learn about Neo4j and graph database management systems, as well as how

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.080
you can benefit from incorporating them into your applications.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:41.440
Next, you will learn how to use a Neo4j graph database management system as the backend

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.440
storage for a real-world application created with Java and Spring Boot.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.960
Finally, you will learn how to create a front-end with React to interact with the data stored

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:52.960
with Neo4j.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.200
Neo4j provided a grant to make this course possible.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.400
So let's get started.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.400
Hi and welcome.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.400
I'm Gavin Lon.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.760
So, I finally got my FreeCoCamp t-shirt.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.760
I traveled all the way from the east coast of South Africa to Amsterdam and met the founder

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.720
of FreeCoCamp, Quincy Larson, and he gave me my own FreeCoCamp t-shirt.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.520
Really great.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.400
So the point of the trip was to meet Quincy and some of the core team members of FreeCoCamp.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.480
In person.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.480
It was about team building and solidifying relationships between coworkers.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.360
The key concept in this course is, in fact, relationships.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.160
Relationships between data.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.880
The relationships could be between people, between car parts, between a t-shirt and a

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.980
human being.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:48.320
So relationships between homogenous data or lack data, for example, between human beings,

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.980
or relationships between heterogenous data or different types of data.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.360
These relationships can be very effectively represented within a graph database management

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.360
system.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.760
Neo4j is a graph database management system.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.040
In this part of the course, I want to present to you a brief overview highlighting the significance

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.680
of a graph database management system.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.360
Before we look at Neo4j, let's contextualize the significance of a graph database management

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.300
system by looking at some of the other popular types of database management systems commonly

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.840
used today.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.040
So let's look at three other commonly used types of database management systems before

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.280
we discuss the graph database management system and why we would want to use a graph database

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.840
management system in our applications.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:40.400
The most well-known used database management system type is a relational database management

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.400
system.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:47.400
The relational database management systems are SQL Server, Postgres, and MySQL.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.920
In this type of database, tables represent entities.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.560
For example, a table named employee contains rows of data.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:58.460
Each row of data is divided into columns or fields containing employee information.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.340
For example, the name field contains the employee name.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.120
The age field contains the employee's age, etc.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.520
Another data entity could be implemented in a table named department.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.220
Each row in the department's table denotes an individual department.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:18.580
For example, one row stores information for the human resources department and another

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.180
row stores information for the finance department.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.240
These data entities or database tables within a relational database can be explicitly related

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.880
to one another within the relational database management system.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:36.100
For example, an entity may have a one-to-one relationship with another entity.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:42.280
So say employees and the cars they drive, a specific employee is related to a specific

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:45.520
car, one employee to one car.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.840
So for example, the employee entity in this case has a one-to-one relationship with the

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.760
car entity.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.720
Two database tables can have a many-to-many relationship.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.720
For example, an employee could be a member of multiple teams.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.520
And each team can contain multiple employees.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.260
So this means an employee can be a member of more than one team, and each team can have

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.060
many employees.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:15.000
So in this scenario, the employee entity has a many-to-many relationship with the team

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.640
entity.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:22.360
A database entity can have a one-to-many relationship with another database entity.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:28.480
For example, the department table has a one-to-many relationship with the employee table, meaning

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.800
each department can contain one or many employees, and an employee can only be a member of one

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.160
department at a given time.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.240
So this one-to-many relationship can be established within the relational database management system

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.240
through an appropriate schema design.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.940
A database schema defines how data is organized within a relational database, which includes

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:56.400
constraints such as table names, fields, data types, and the relationships between the relevant

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.880
entities.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:01.840
So for example, the one-to-many relationship between the department table and the employee

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:08.280
table can be implemented by including a primary key, which is a unique identifier for a row

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:14.360
of data in the department table, and a foreign key field within the employee table, which

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:18.540
relates to the primary key value within the department table.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.960
The main advantages of a relational database design is that it helps facilitate data integrity,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.380
so the data is not easily corrupted.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:32.660
As well as reduces data redundancy, storage for repeated values are reduced.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:38.320
For example, if you stored department data within the employee table, you can see here,

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.360
the department short name field and long name field values are repeated across rows in the

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:49.320
employee table, whereas separating the data into an employee table and a department table

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:55.240
means these values are not repeated, resulting in saving storage space.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:01.440
As the data scales, this storage saving can be quite significant.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.880
A technique known as normalization is employed to design a relational database.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.440
I won't go into the details of normalization here, but please look up normalization, which

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.480
is a key concept in relational database design.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:18.140
So the technique of normalization can increase data integrity, minimizing data corruption

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:24.440
and also significantly reducing data redundancy, i.e. unnecessarily storing values repeatedly.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.960
The downside to relational databases is the speed of retrieving data when multiple joins

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.840
or deep joins between entities are necessary.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:39.520
For example, if you want to retrieve analytical data that summarizes data from multiple entities,

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:45.040
a normalized database design can cause the retrieval of such data to be a slow process.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.800
So the way to speed up data retrieval can be to use a schema-less database management

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.560
system like, for example, MongoDB.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.940
This is a document-based database management system.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:00.680
The fact that the database design is not bound by a schema like with a relational database

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:06.960
management system can result in increased speed of data retrieval as well as allow for

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.060
better design flexibility.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.000
So you can see that each of these database management systems have their purpose and

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.280
advantages.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.080
Another type of database management system may use a name-value pair storage structure

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.580
like, for example, Redis.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:27.280
This database management system is great for caching frequently retrieved data and therefore

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.400
can help speed up a system.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.120
So we have briefly looked at three common types of database management systems.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.120
Relational database management systems like, for example, SQL Server or Postgres, schema-less

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:45.680
database management systems like, for example, MongoDB, and name-value pair database management

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:47.720
systems like Redis.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.600
So let's look at the significance of a graph database management system.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:55.080
Neo4j is a graph database management system.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:59.040
This type of database management system is based on the graph data structure.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:05.020
All of these social media platforms use graph databases, Twitter, Facebook, and Instagram.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:08.400
As we all know, these platforms have millions of users.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.800
A relational database management system would not be ideal as the only storage facility

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.460
for such data.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.960
Because of the way the data is structured would mean multiple joints between entities

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.560
would need to be made.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.360
When relevance data is retrieved, which would drastically slow down the data retrieval process

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:32.200
ultimately causing a poor UX user experience because the whole system is slowed down by

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:35.260
these joints that need to be made between data.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:40.560
So note that when designing a particular system like a social media platform, you could incorporate

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.120
both a graph database management system and a relational database management system into

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.320
your overall system design.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:52.480
This type of solution could combine the enforced data integrity inherent in relational database

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.760
management systems with the complex relationships between data entities or nodes that can be

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.240
facilitated using a graph database management system.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:05.960
For example, the master database could be a relational database where data integrity

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:10.760
and data redundancy concerns are addressed by the inherent benefits that we have discussed

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.760
regarding a relational database management system.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.800
A graph database management system that is appropriately synchronized with the relevant

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.640
relational database management system can be leveraged to increase the retrieval speed

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.720
of data that, for example, has complex relationships.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.600
If data that requires complex relationships was retrieved directly from the relational

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.720
database management system, the joints required when retrieving the relevant data could significantly

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:42.180
slow down the system and ultimately result in a very poor UX user experience.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:46.660
So by including a graph database management system in the overall system where it sits

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:51.760
on top of the relational database management system, as it were, and is used to speed up

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:56.520
the retrieval of data with complex relationships, this could significantly speed up the data

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:01.320
retrieval process resulting in a far better UX user experience.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.600
So this sort of system would require that the data stored in the relevant graph database

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:10.460
management system and the relevant relational database management system be synchronized

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:15.120
so as to ensure that the data is consistent across the two disparate database management

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.120
systems.

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:20.640
So a system like this, when designed properly, can leverage the benefits of both types of

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.760
database management systems.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:28.300
For graph-based data structures, a database management system that focuses on the facilitation

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:33.040
of relationships between entities or nodes is essential, and a graph database management

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.640
system provides a way to store data based on the graph data structure, which is ideal

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:44.000
for applications that consume data where entities have multiple relationships and complex relationships

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.760
with one another.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:50.480
Think about social media platforms where you have relationships between users and posts,

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:55.160
likes, friendships, etc., and you have millions of people using these platforms.

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:59.840
A database management system is needed that appropriately facilitates the storage of data

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:02.480
for a large number of different types of relationships.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:07.760
A graph database management system allows for relationships to be stored as data within

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.600
the graph database.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:14.160
With a relational database management system, the emphasis is on the entities, and generally

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.640
the relationships between the entities are not stored in the database, but rather general

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.480
relationships are established at the design level.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:26.840
For example, you can see by the foreign key field value that the employee is a member

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:32.960
of the HR department, but there are no details about the relationship itself between the

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:37.800
relevant department and employee, saved in the database.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:42.700
This is something you could easily do within a graph database management system.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:46.380
You could create a more detailed relationship.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:51.000
You can easily save data regarding individual relationships between entities or nodes, for

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:55.880
example between an employee node and a department node.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:12:00.440
So here is a whiteboard presentation that shows how the relationships between individual

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:06.880
employees and employees and departments could be represented within a graph database.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.560
So here we see a node that represents a department.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:13.800
This node is appropriately labeled department.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:18.360
Details related to this department node can be saved to the database as properties and

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.200
related values.

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:25.180
So this department contains a short name property with a value of HR, and a long name property

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:28.560
with a value of human resources.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.440
Here is our first employee node.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:33.600
This node is appropriately labeled employee.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:38.520
The employee node contains properties and relevant values representing the employee's

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:40.720
name and age.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.800
Here we see our first relationship.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:48.560
This relationship explicitly shows that the employee whose name is Brad Jenkins is part

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:50.720
of the HR department.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:54.400
This relationship is appropriately labeled member of.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.720
So you can see that this is a very human readable representation of data.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:04.560
The fact that this employee is a member of the HR department is clear through appropriate

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.940
labeling and properties.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:10.880
So Brad Jenkins is a member of the HR department.

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:14.680
We are able to add properties to the relationships themselves.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:21.700
So the member of relationship has a join date property as well as an employee type property.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:27.080
You can see by this data when this employee joined the department and that this employee

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:31.120
has a permanent role and is not for example a contractor.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:35.760
Here we have the second employee node who has the name Jenny Lane.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:39.200
Jenny is also a member of the HR department.

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:43.600
We can save relationships between employees within our graph database.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.360
So Jenny works with Brad.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:51.320
We can also create another works with relationship in the opposite direction where Brad works

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:53.080
with Jenny.

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:57.740
So you can see that we have a one department to many employees relationship just like what

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:02.540
was shown earlier in the relational database but a graph database is far more flexible

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:07.900
in terms of design when establishing relationships between data points or nodes.

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:12.740
Not only can the entities contain associated data but the relationships themselves can

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:18.600
also contain data i.e. through properties and associated values.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.820
So we could create even more complex relationships between nodes.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:28.220
For example a chain of command hierarchical structure related to our employee nodes.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:33.660
So here is an example of this where employee James Stokes reports to another employee Jenny

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:34.660
Lane.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:39.500
This is just a simple example and of course this hierarchical data structure could become

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.560
far more complex.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:46.520
So this presentation was only intended to give you a basic idea of how much more flexible

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:51.660
the design of relationships between entities can be within a graph database and how complex

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:58.820
relationships between entities can be easily implemented within a graph database.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:03.860
I want to show you a brief demonstration of using Cypher to query a graph database and

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:08.140
to do this I'll firstly create a Neo4j database on my local machine.

00:15:08.140 --> 00:15:10.820
I'll use my Mac platform to demonstrate this.

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:17.400
I'll contrast using Cypher and Neo4j with using SQL in the SQL Server database which

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:20.340
of course is a relational database management system.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:21.920
Firstly what is Cypher?

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:26.000
Cypher can be likened to SQL or Structured Query Language.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:31.380
Cypher is a text-based declarative language used for querying a Neo4j database.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.580
What is meant by a declarative language?

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:39.420
A declarative language is a high level language that describes what a computation should perform.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:43.980
These languages must follow strict semantic rules, must be structured in a particular

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:45.220
way.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:49.900
In order to be valid and for desired results to be returned from the relevant database

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:55.300
management system, for example using SQL in order to bring back all employee records from

00:15:55.300 --> 00:16:00.940
the employee table, you would appropriately execute the following SQL code.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:08.760
This SQL statement simply returns all rows and all columns from the employee table.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:14.320
Using Cypher if you wanted to return all employee data, you would use a query like this.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:19.620
This query would return all employee nodes and relevant relationships.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:24.460
Although when comparing these two query languages, they are of course semantically different,

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:29.500
but principally they are the same in that the relevant declarative text-based code is

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:35.420
parsed by the appropriate database management system converted into appropriate instructions

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:38.060
that are executed under the hood as it were.

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:41.820
The relevant results are then returned from the relevant database.

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:45.660
I'd like to demonstrate using Cypher within a Neo4j database.

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:51.660
I'll contrast executing Cypher against the Neo4j database with executing similar queries

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:56.580
against a similar database implemented in a SQL Server database.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:17:00.140
You don't need to follow along with this part of the course, but of course you can if you

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:01.380
want to.

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:06.020
So if you want to follow along, you can install Neo4j on your Mac or Windows platforms by

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:08.460
following the instructions at this URL.

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:12.460
I have already installed Neo4j on my Mac platform.

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:17.580
If you want to install a free version of SQL Server, please navigate to this URL.

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:22.060
But as discussed, this part of the course is only intended to be an overview and not

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:26.940
a detailed explanation, so it's not necessary to follow along with this part of the course.

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:31.860
I've created these scripts on GitHub for this part of the demonstration, so I'm going to

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:38.620
copy this script from GitHub and run it in Neo4j to generate my Neo4j graph database.

