This is the type of relationship this user is going to have with the course.

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:02.500
So what we are doing, we are matching for a user and a course where the username is

00:41:02.500 --> 00:41:05.940
for HAN and course identifier is CPP.

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:08.260
Then we are creating a relationship.

00:41:08.260 --> 00:41:10.660
What kind of relationship you say?

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:14.840
We are creating an outgoing relationship from the user.

00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:21.140
And how do I know that, as you can see, there is an arrow going out from the user towards

00:41:21.140 --> 00:41:22.380
the course.

00:41:22.380 --> 00:41:27.260
So this is an outgoing relationship from the user.

00:41:27.260 --> 00:41:29.300
And what is the type of this relationship?

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:33.200
The type is actually enrolled in.

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:40.460
Now we can actually save this relationship inside a variable called R.

00:41:40.460 --> 00:41:46.860
And I can say return R.

00:41:46.860 --> 00:41:56.020
So if I press the play button again, it just looks like I have successfully created a relationship.

00:41:56.020 --> 00:42:04.460
So the relationship actually starts at node 1 and ends at node 4, which means that the

00:42:04.460 --> 00:42:10.020
ID of HAN has been showed here is 1 and ID of CPP is 4.

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:15.780
If I go to user, you can see for HAN has been showed here has the ID 1.

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:23.220
And if I go to course, C++ course has the ID of 4.

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:28.060
Okay and the type of relationship is enrolled in, there are no properties whatsoever.

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:31.660
There are the element ID, start node element ID and things like that.

00:42:31.660 --> 00:42:34.260
We do not have to worry about them.

00:42:34.260 --> 00:42:40.060
So that's how you can create a relationship between these two nodes.

00:42:40.060 --> 00:42:45.700
So that's how you can create relationships inside of Neo4j.

00:42:45.700 --> 00:42:49.680
Now let's see how you can update a property of a node.

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:57.060
So if I want to update the name of John to John Doe and John to John Doe, I can do that.

00:42:57.060 --> 00:43:06.060
First we have to find John in our database, so we will send match a variable u of type

00:43:06.060 --> 00:43:14.960
user where u.username equals John.

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:20.900
So now we are matching for a user with the username John.

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:27.700
Then we want to use set, this is another clause in Neo4j cipher language.

00:43:27.700 --> 00:43:38.980
So I will say set u.name equals John Doe.

00:43:38.980 --> 00:43:45.260
And if I execute this query, looks like it has done its job.

00:43:45.260 --> 00:43:56.140
So if I go back and instead of setting it, if I return u, John has now become John Doe.

00:43:56.140 --> 00:44:03.020
We can do the same thing with Jane because why not, they are related.

00:44:03.020 --> 00:44:08.740
Jane and we can say Jane Doe.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:16.860
Okay, so I want to return Jane, return u.

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:17.860
There you have it.

00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:21.480
So now you have learned how to match nodes in the database.

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:27.340
You have learned how to create new relationships, update values of an existing node.

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:32.060
Now that's pretty much all you are going to need for the API development process.

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:37.460
But I would like to show you one last clause from the cipher query language.

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:41.060
And that is the exist clause.

00:44:41.060 --> 00:44:49.060
For example, if you ever want to check if Farhan has introduced is enrolled in the C++

00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:54.580
course or not, you can do that using the exist clause.

00:44:54.580 --> 00:44:58.100
So we will begin with match and what do we want to match?

00:44:58.100 --> 00:45:10.780
We want to match for an user and we want to match for a course where u.username is equals

00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:19.860
Farhan and c.identifier is equals cpp.

00:45:19.860 --> 00:45:29.020
Now we have an user with the username Farhan and we also have a course with the identifier

00:45:29.020 --> 00:45:30.020
cpp.

00:45:30.020 --> 00:45:41.140
Next, what we want to do is we want to say return and we will call the exists function.

00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:45.820
This is a function and that's why we have these two parentheses and inside these parentheses

00:45:45.820 --> 00:45:48.900
we want to describe a relationship.

00:45:48.900 --> 00:46:03.340
So the relationship we are looking for or checking for is whether user is enrolled in

00:46:03.340 --> 00:46:07.220
the course or not.

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:08.580
So we will finish our query.

00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:12.940
What we are doing, we are matching for a user with the username Farhan and we are matching

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:15.820
for a course with the identifier cpp.

00:46:15.820 --> 00:46:21.220
Then we are checking whether a relationship between them of type enrolled in exists or

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:22.220
not.

00:46:22.220 --> 00:46:27.540
So I will press the play button and it says true.

00:46:27.540 --> 00:46:36.700
Now if I switch cpp for something else like JS, we have the JavaScript course and I hit

00:46:36.700 --> 00:46:42.060
the play button, you will see I am getting false.

