04:38:27.000 --> 04:38:30.960
Let's create a hook named Use Axios Private.

04:38:30.960 --> 04:38:36.120
So the code for the hook is created so that the client code can retrieve an appropriate

04:38:36.120 --> 04:38:42.480
Axios client object that automatically includes an appropriate HTTP header containing the

04:38:42.480 --> 04:38:44.920
logged-on user's credentials.

04:38:44.920 --> 04:38:49.320
These credentials are appropriately formatted for basic authentication.

04:38:49.320 --> 04:38:55.100
So the returned Axios client object from this hook must be used for accessing protected

04:38:55.100 --> 04:39:01.100
resources on the server, i.e. retrieved through the relevant API endpoints.

04:39:01.100 --> 04:39:06.840
These protected resources require basic authentication to be performed before returning relevant

04:39:06.840 --> 04:39:10.360
data to the client.

04:39:10.360 --> 04:39:15.880
Now the code here creates what's known as an interceptor, so if the Use Axios Private

04:39:15.880 --> 04:39:21.000
hook is used from within client code to reference the Axios client, the login credentials are

04:39:21.000 --> 04:39:26.420
automatically injected into the header of the relevant HTTP request.

04:39:26.420 --> 04:39:31.000
So this means that the client code doesn't need to inject this authentication header

04:39:31.000 --> 04:39:36.200
information every time a HTTP request is made to an endpoint.

04:39:36.200 --> 04:39:39.640
This interceptor code does this automatically.

