04:40:39.920 --> 04:40:45.480
to easily access the auth variable so that the relevant component can access whether

04:40:45.480 --> 04:40:50.400
the user is logged into the system or not logged into the system.

04:40:50.400 --> 04:40:52.680
So let's look at how this works.

04:40:52.680 --> 04:40:55.440
Let's open the home.js file.

04:40:55.440 --> 04:41:09.040
Let's import the use auth hook.

04:41:09.040 --> 04:41:14.800
Let's use the use auth hook to return the auth variable and the set auth function from

04:41:14.800 --> 04:41:26.240
the relevant context global store.

04:41:26.240 --> 04:41:31.640
Now within the UI.jsx code section of the home component, we can check the auth variable

04:41:31.640 --> 04:41:38.680
to see if the user is logged into the system, like this.

04:41:38.680 --> 04:41:42.720
So if the user is logged into the system, the code displays a message on the screen.

04:41:42.720 --> 04:41:43.960
You are logged in.

04:41:43.960 --> 04:41:49.580
If the user is not logged into the system, the code displays the hero image to the screen.

04:41:49.580 --> 04:41:56.120
So let's open the login.js file and use the use auth hook to return the set auth function

04:41:56.120 --> 04:42:06.880
from the context.

04:42:06.880 --> 04:42:11.200
Let's use the set auth function to assign an appropriate object to the auth variable.

04:42:11.200 --> 04:42:17.680
This object contains the username and password.

04:42:17.680 --> 04:42:23.400
So once the user is successfully authenticated, an object containing the user's username

04:42:23.400 --> 04:42:29.400
and password is passed into the set auth function, which effectively changes the state of the

04:42:29.400 --> 04:42:35.360
auth variable, which indicates, of course, that the user is logged into the system.

04:42:35.360 --> 04:42:41.000
So you can see that now this global context can be used to retrieve the username and password

04:42:41.000 --> 04:42:46.800
from this object, ie the object that we have just passed in to the set auth function to

04:42:46.800 --> 04:42:49.440
change the state of the auth variable.

04:42:49.440 --> 04:42:55.680
The username and password can now be injected into the header of HTTP requests through the

04:42:55.680 --> 04:43:02.560
interceptor functionality that we have implemented in the use Axios private hook.

